# Client-style speaking drills (prep 1 minute, speak 1 minute). The model is a strong 45-60 second answer.
DRILLS = []
def drill(id, scenario, pis, model):
    DRILLS.append(dict(id=id, scenario=scenario, pis=pis, model=model, style="client"))

drill("d-why-invest","A client (judge) says: \"I keep my money in savings because the stock market is basically gambling. Why should I invest?\"",
 ["FI:270","FI:077"],
 "Saving and investing do different jobs. Savings is for safety and emergencies, and you should keep three to six months there. But money that sits at a low rate loses buying power to inflation every year. Investing in a diversified index fund isn't betting on one company; you own a slice of hundreds of businesses. Since 1928 the S&P 500 has averaged roughly 10% a year, with bad years along the way, so it's for money you won't need for at least five years. Gambling has a negative expected return; owning businesses has historically had a positive one.")
drill("d-automate","A client (judge) says: \"I always mean to invest, but at the end of the month there's nothing left.\" Recommend a fix.",
 ["FI:270","FI:065"],
 "The fix is to stop relying on willpower and automate it. Pay yourself first: set up an automatic transfer the day after payday. Brokerages like Fidelity, Schwab and Vanguard let you schedule recurring investments into an S&P 500 index fund for as little as a few dollars, and many offer fractional shares. Start with an amount you won't miss, like 5% of take-home pay, and raise it 1% every time you get a raise. Dollar-cost averaging means you buy more shares when prices dip. In a year you'll have invested without ever deciding to.")
drill("d-fiduciary","A client (judge) asks: \"How do I know you're working for me and not for your company?\"",
 ["CR:012","EI:128"],
 "That's exactly the right question. First, we'll tell you in writing how we're paid, whether that's a flat fee, a percentage of assets or commissions, so you can see any conflicts. Second, we're held to a best-interest standard: our recommendations must fit your goals, risk tolerance and time horizon, and we document why. Third, you can verify us yourself: look us up on FINRA BrokerCheck or the SEC's adviser search to see licenses and any complaints. And you'll always get our recommendation and the reasons, so the decision stays yours.")
drill("d-term-whole","A new parent (judge) asks: \"Should I buy term or whole life insurance?\"",
 ["FI:571","FI:081"],
 "For most young families, term life is the better fit. It covers you for a set period, like 20 or 30 years, which matches the years your child depends on you, and it costs a fraction of whole life for the same coverage. A common rule of thumb is coverage of 10 to 12 times your income. Whole life lasts forever and builds cash value, but premiums can be many times higher. Many planners suggest buying term and investing the difference in a retirement account. We'd also add disability insurance, since you're more likely to miss work from injury than to die young.")
drill("d-fdic-sipc","A client (judge) asks: \"If my bank or brokerage goes under, do I lose everything?\"",
 ["FI:336","RM:043"],
 "No, there are two safety nets. At a bank, the FDIC insures deposits up to $250,000 per depositor, per bank, per ownership category, and credit unions get the same through the NCUA. In 2023, when Silicon Valley Bank failed, insured depositors kept every dollar. At a brokerage, SIPC protects up to $500,000, including $250,000 in cash, if the firm itself fails. What SIPC doesn't do is protect you from the market going down. So the plan is: keep deposits under the limits at each bank and spread large balances across institutions.")
drill("d-roth-trad","A 19-year-old with a part-time job (judge) asks: \"Roth IRA or traditional IRA?\"",
 ["FI:569","BL:134"],
 "At 19, the Roth is usually the winner. You pay tax on the money now, while your tax rate is low, and then every dollar of growth comes out tax-free in retirement. The limit is $7,500 for 2026 and 2027, but you can only contribute up to what you earned. A traditional IRA gives you a deduction now, which matters more when you're in a higher bracket. Bonus: Roth contributions, not earnings, can come out any time, so it doubles as flexible savings. Even $50 a month at 19 could grow to well over $150,000 by retirement.")
drill("d-credit-score","A client (judge) asks: \"How can I raise my credit score fast? I want to buy a car in six months.\"",
 ["FI:071","FI:072"],
 "There are two levers that move fastest. First is utilization, which is about 30% of your FICO score: pay balances down so you use under 30% of your limits, and under 10% is even better. Second is payment history, the biggest factor at about 35%: put every bill on autopay so nothing is ever late. Pull your free reports at AnnualCreditReport.com and dispute any errors. Don't open or close cards right before applying. In six months, those steps can make a real difference, and that can save you hundreds on the loan.")
drill("d-avalanche","A client (judge) has four credit cards and asks which to pay off first.",
 ["FI:568","FI:782"],
 "Make the minimum on every card so nothing goes late, then throw every extra dollar at one card at a time. The math says go highest interest rate first; that's the avalanche method and it saves the most money. If motivation is the problem, the snowball method pays off the smallest balance first for a quick win. Either works if you stick with it. Also call each card company and ask for a lower rate, since many will say yes. And once a card is paid off, keep it open with a small autopaid bill to protect your score.")
drill("d-529","A grandparent (judge) wants to help pay for a newborn grandchild's college. What do you recommend?",
 ["FI:831","FI:062"],
 "A 529 plan is the strongest tool. The money grows tax-free when it's used for qualified education costs, and many states give a state tax deduction. Starting at birth gives 18 years of compounding: $100 a month at 6% grows to almost $39,000. If the child gets a scholarship or skips college, you can change the beneficiary to another family member, and under SECURE 2.0 up to $35,000 can roll into the child's Roth IRA. For 2026, you can give up to $19,000 a year per person without filing a gift tax return.")
drill("d-recession","A nervous client (judge) says: \"Everyone says a recession is coming. Should I sell everything?\"",
 ["FI:574","EI:134"],
 "I understand the worry, but selling everything usually turns a temporary drop into a permanent loss. Nobody times the market consistently, and some of the best days often come right after the worst. The S&P 500 fell about 37% in 2008 and fully recovered within a few years for people who stayed invested. What we can control is your plan: keep an emergency fund so you never have to sell in a downturn, make sure your stock and bond mix matches your timeline, and keep investing monthly so you buy at lower prices. Following the herd is how people buy high and sell low.")
drill("d-sole-llc","A client (judge) starting a dog-grooming business asks whether to be a sole proprietor or an LLC.",
 ["BL:003","EN:039"],
 "A sole proprietorship is the simplest: no filing in most states and you keep all the profit, but you're personally liable, so if a dog is injured and you're sued, your house and savings are at risk. An LLC costs a small state filing fee but separates your personal assets from business debts and lawsuits, and profits still pass through to your personal tax return. Since grooming involves animals and customer property, I'd recommend an LLC plus general liability insurance. Open a separate business bank account so the protection holds up.")
drill("d-break-even","A coffee cart owner (judge) has $4,000 in monthly fixed costs. Each drink sells for $5 and costs $1.80 to make. How many drinks to break even, and what does it mean?",
 ["FI:106","OP:024"],
 "Each drink contributes $5 minus $1.80, which is $3.20, toward fixed costs. $4,000 divided by $3.20 is 1,250 drinks a month, about 57 a day over 22 working days. Below that you lose money; every drink above it is profit. To lower the break-even, you can raise prices slightly, cut the cost per drink with bulk buying, or reduce fixed costs like rent. I'd track daily sales against that 57-drink target every day.")
drill("d-inflation","A client (judge) asks: \"Why does inflation matter for my savings?\"",
 ["EC:083","FI:062"],
 "Inflation is a hidden tax on cash. If prices rise 3.7% a year, as they did in the year through September 2026, $10,000 in an account paying 0.25% buys about $345 less a year later. Over ten years at 3% inflation, money sitting still loses about a quarter of its buying power. That's why you keep only emergency money in basic savings, use high-yield savings or CDs paying around 4% for short-term goals, and invest long-term money in assets like stocks that historically beat inflation.")
drill("d-scam","A client (judge) received a text saying their bank account is locked and to click a link. What do you tell them?",
 ["FI:073","FI:830"],
 "Don't click it. That's a phishing scam, and texts like this are one of the most common ways criminals steal logins. Real banks won't ask you to verify your account through a link or ask for a one-time passcode. Instead, open the bank's app directly or call the number on the back of your card. To protect yourself going forward, turn on multifactor authentication with an authenticator app, set alerts for every transaction, and freeze your credit with all three bureaus for free. If you did click, change your password immediately and call the bank.")
drill("d-cfp-career","The judge, a bank HR director, asks your team: \"What finance careers fit someone who loves helping people and is good with numbers?\"",
 ["PD:152","PD:153"],
 "Personal financial planning is a great match: you help families with budgets, investing, insurance and retirement, and the CFP certification is the gold standard. Other options are credit counselor, loan officer, private banker and insurance agent. If you like numbers more than client work, look at financial analyst or accountant, with the CPA. To get started: join DECA and FBLA, take an internship or volunteer with a free tax-prep program, and network through informational interviews. Building relationships early matters because most advisors grow through referrals.")
